## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect cross-repo blue/green deployments with per-env isolation and safe promotion gates, using remote state data sources across repos.

## Key Concepts
- Blue/Green deployment across environments
- Terraform data sources and remote state cross-repo access
- Per-env backends and promotion gating in CI
- Route53 weighted routing for traffic shifting

## Code Example
```javascript
data "terraform_remote_state" "network" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = { bucket = "infra-network-terraform" key = "prod/terraform.tfstate" region = "us-east-1" }
}
resource "aws_lb_target_group" "blue" { }
resource "aws_lb_target_group" "green" { }
resource "aws_route53_record" "prod" {
  name = "app-prod.example.com"
  type = "A"
  set_identifier = "prod-blue"
  weight = 100
  alias { name = aws_lb_prod.dns_name, zone_id = aws_lb_prod.zone_id, evaluate_target_health = true }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift if network outputs change?
- How do you automate health checks before promoting traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-cloud, multi-env orchestration, and robust gating. It probes backend scoping, provider aliasing, and policy-as-code integration in a realistic production-like flow.

## Key Concepts
- Per-env Terraform backends and state isolation
- Provider aliasing for multi-cloud deployments
- Policy-as-code gates (Sentinel/OPA) for prod safety
- Wrapper modules for consistent tagging across clouds

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend.tf (Terraform S3 backend per environment)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "org-terraform"
    key    = "state/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Providers with aliases (multi-cloud)
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  alias  = "aws"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp"
}
```

```javascript
// Wrapper module usage for tags
module "shared_tags" {
  source      = "./modules/tags"
  environment = var.environment
  cost_center = var.cost_center
}
```

```javascript
// Simple OPA/rego style policy (example)
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.environment != "prod"
}
```

```javascript
// CI gate steps (high level)
terraform init
terraform plan -out=tfplan
terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json
# Evaluate plan.json against policy (opa or sentinel)
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-repo data sharing and multi-region provider patterns, plus a practical gating approach to prevent drift.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across regions
- data.terraform_remote_state for read-only cross-repo data
- Per-region backends and workflows
- CI policy gates to enforce drift checks and approvals
- Cross-region resource referencing of VPC/subnet IDs

## Code Example
```javascript
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.3"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "east"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
data "terraform_remote_state" "net" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "infra-network-bucket"
    key    = "network/us-east-1/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
# usage
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  provider    = aws.west
  ami         = "ami-0abcdef1234567890"
  subnet_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.net.outputs.public_subnet_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across repos? 
- How would you handle regional failover and state restoration?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a cloud-agnostic Terraform module with clean separation of concerns, provider aliasing, and robust gating to prevent drift or accidental exposure.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliases and multi-cloud orchestration
- Module abstraction over provider-specific resources
- Per-environment backends and key management
- CI plan gating with JSON plan validation
- Basic test coverage via Terratest for existence and config

## Code Example

```terraform
# Root usage
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" region = "us-east-1" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" project = var.environment }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" features = {} }

module "object_store" {
  source = "./modules/object_store"

  cloud       = var.cloud
  bucket_name = var.bucket_name
  versioning  = var.versioning
  public_read = var.public_read
  environment = var.environment
}
```

```terraform
# modules/object_store/main.tf (skeleton)
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" }

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  count = var.cloud == "aws" ? 1 : 0
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = var.public_read ? "public-read" : "private"

  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

# google and azurerm blocks would mirror the above with appropriate attributes
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to support policy-driven controls (e.g., CFRs) across clouds?
- How would you test drift between environments and prevent accidental exposure in prod?
